00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:17.170
{=6}When we grow up

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:20.090
Florist!

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:20.970
Fire Fighter!

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:22.100
Elephant!

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:23.390
Yep, yep.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:26.060
I want to be like you, Uramichi Oniisan!

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:33.400
Because you're cool and kind,
and you work very hard!

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:40.030
What's wrong, Oniisan?

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:41.450
Are you crying?

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:43.030
What? Is he crying for real?

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:46.040
Uramichi Oniisan!

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:46.120
When we grow up

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:50.080
What do you want to be
when you grow up, Oniisan?

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:54.380
I've already grown,
so I won't grow any more.

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:55.750
Poor thing.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:13:00.720
What should I do if my dream
doesn't come true?

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:02.140
When you have a dream,

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:07.640
the longer you hold onto it,
the heavier it gets.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:09.480
There are people who can keep sight

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.650
of where they belong
by keeping a hold of their dream.

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:16.110
There are also people
who feel at ease by letting it go.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:20.610
So, I think it's okay to keep holding
the dreams you kids have now.

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:25.030
It's also okay for you
to leave it somewhere.

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:28.370
Okay, then! We'll sing a song
that fits today's topic now!

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:36.420
The Free Sample Tasted So Good, So Why
Was It Just Normal When I Got It Home?

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:32.000
"The Free Sample Tasted So Good,

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:36.210
So Why Was It Just Normal
When I Got It Home?"

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:44.180
♬ I finished work earlier than usual ♬

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:46.640
♬ And dropped into a bakery on a whim ♬

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:49.520
♬ The clerk there let me try a sample ♬

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:54.020
♬ When I walk this road normally,
the streetlights are on ♬

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:55.310
♬ It's different today ♬

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:58.150
♬ There's still a little sun ♬

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:01.740
♬ This is the giddiest
I've been all month ♬

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:06.780
♬ Just an ordinary,
everyday chunk of salted bread ♬

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:12.910
♬ I thought it was ambrosia or something ♬

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:11.240
"Sst"

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:16.630
♬ I really did ♬

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:21.130
♬ I ended up buying two of them ♬

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:24.050
♬ The clerk's smile was so dazzling ♬

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:26.470
♬ I had to pretend ♬

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:28.930
♬ That when I got home ♬

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:31.350
♬ I wouldn't be all alone ♬

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.890
♬ Why? (Why?) ♬

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:38.820
♬ Back home, it was just
completely normal bread ♬

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:40.860
♬ It wasn't ambrosia ♬

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:48.620
♬ But just normal bread
(Just normal bread) ♬

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:51.160
♬ Where did it go? ♬

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:58.540
♬ The world that seemed to glitter,
my heart all aflutter ♬

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:03.380
♬ Why is my place so gloomy? ♬

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:07.970
♬ I only changed the lightbulb yesterday ♬

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:10.560
♬ But deep down, I knew why ♬

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:19.150
♬ It wasn't the bread,
it was how I felt inside ♬

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:21.980
♬ When I next get off early ♬

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:24.030
♬ I'll go back to the bakery ♬

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:26.910
♬ 'Cause even if it's
just a fleeting dream ♬

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:29.370
♬ I long to once more see
the world all agleam ♬

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:31.740
♬ La la la la la la, la la la la la ♬

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:34.250
♬ La la la la la la, la la ♬

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.750
♬ La la la la la la la, la la la la ♬

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:41.750
Okay! That's good!

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:43.550
Thank you for your good work!

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:49.970
Uramichi-<i>kun</i>. You did
a great job again today.

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:52.600
Yeah... Thank you.

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:58.810
Uramichi Oniisan!

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:00.810
We meet again.

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.570
Wh-What're you doing here?

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:08.740
Well, I thought I'd observe
the shoot for a change.

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:10.530
I'm going back to my office now.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:12.330
Good-bye!

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:15.870
Uramichi Oniisan!

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:16.910
Hm?

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:18.420
Oh... You're from earlier...

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:21.000
Look! It's God of Shiso!

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.210
My mom's old friend is making it.

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:26.550
He gave it to me!

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:29.180
He told me not to tell other kids!

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:31.930
Kikaku-<i>san</i>...

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:35.720
Uramichi Oniisan.

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:38.600
{=7}IZAKAYA

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:43.110
Hey, listen! Nekota!

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.110
Hm?

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:48.780
Uramichi-<i>san </i>had me run some errands
for him, but he got really angry at me.

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:51.070
It's so unfair!

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:52.740
It's true that I'm not completely innocent

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:55.540
since I went to pachinko
with the change from the errands.

00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:59.040
But I did buy a <i>taiyaki</i>
and gave it to him.

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:04.130
Hey, Nekota. I'm not
that much at fault, right?

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:07.050
You're not at fault at all, Usahara.

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:08.540
Manager of Bar 'Cat Kick'

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:08.540
Matahiko Nekota (28)

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:10.680
Right?!

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:11.510
You're hurting me.

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:14.720
I knew you'd say that.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:16.100
When I told Kumatani about it, he said,

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:17.970
"You're completely at fault."

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:20.730
Can you believe him?!

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:25.770
At any rate, it's amazing that you guys
are like <i>that </i>Uramichi-<i>san</i>'s friends.

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:29.440
I'm jealous. I wish I had
been his roommate, too.

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:32.450
But he became our roommate
because you left in the first place.

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:35.080
But living with my girlfriend
would be more exciting

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:37.950
than living in a dorm full of guys, right?

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:41.710
It's about time for you guys to
get married at least once or twice.

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:45.290
Fundamentally speaking,
it should be once! Just once!

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:47.670
It's not funny when
a two-time divorcee says that.

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:52.260
I have to earn money to pay child support
for two kids, so drink like a fish.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:58.770
Speaking of Uramichi-<i>san</i>, there were
many rumors about him, weren't there?

00:17:58.770 --> 00:17:59.720
Rumors?

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:03.900
You know, that he knocked out an alligator
bare-handed at an outdoor training camp.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.770
Or that he had a fan club
at a nearby girls' school.

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:10.570
Fan club? No way!
I'd be damned if they did!

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:12.400
That's more likely
than the alligator story.

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:14.280
The alligator story is more likely!

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.870
We pretty much don't
have alligators in Japan.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:20.750
What?! I said talking to you
was a waste of time, didn't I?!

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:18.410
Hm?

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.790
Do you wanna settle this
in court, you bastard?!

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:25.830
What?! What the hell are you-- Hm?!

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:29.080
Damn merchandise sales bastard
(Damn Bastard)

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:29.420
He hung up on me.

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:32.090
Argh... That damn bastard!

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:35.930
I wanna crack his skull
into pieces and make a stew.

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:38.850
Damn bastard!

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:41.310
Tequila. Double.

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:42.180
Here you go.

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:43.480
That was quick!

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:50.730
Ack!

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:55.950
Ah! Usao-<i>kun</i>-<i>san</i>!

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:57.820
He knows me!

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:03.040
I'm Kikaku from MHK Enterprise!

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:05.580
Maybe he's being so close
because of the tequila!

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:06.960
Hey, Kumatani!

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:09.960
Oh, hey, Nekota. When we
went to find a <i>tsuchinoko</i>,

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.960
Kuma-- What? Hey...!

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:12.710
You're kidding me, right?
You're ignoring me in this situation?

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:12.710
we only found woodlice, remember?

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:16.220
--Nice to meet you. Here! This is my card.
--Seriously?! Really?! Are you kidding?!

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:16.220
Oh, yeah. It was during the summer break.

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:19.180
When we moved the rocks,
there were so many--

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:17.340
Hey!

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:21.060
Hey! Don't leave me out
in the cold! Kumatani! Hey!

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:21.060
Hm?

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:23.220
Who are you?

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:26.270
Dammit! I feel like I might
lose faith in humanity!

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.940
Please don't touch me.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:33.740
I want to hear about your life
vision and such, Usao-<i>kun</i>-<i>san</i>.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:36.740
That's definitely not something
I'd discuss at our first meeting.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:38.200
Maybe it's because of the tequila,

00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:42.040
but the way he tries to become
closer with others isn't right!

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:44.960
Uramichi Oniisan!

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:48.460
You're good friends with
Uramichi Oniisan, huh?

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:53.380
We are good friends... or rather,
we've known each other for a long time.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:55.130
Even if you know someone very well,

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:58.430
there are some things about
them that you don't know.

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:04.220
Come to think of it, I saw Uramichi-<i>san</i>
at T**s"R"Us the other day.

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:05.770
What? Why was he...?

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:08.480
I thought about saying hello, but...

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:15.280
...he was checking dress-up dolls
for girls very seriously.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:19.700
He was buying a super frilly,
cute dress, too.

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:22.490
I felt like I saw something
I shouldn't have.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:29.170
Well, we don't have the right
to criticize his personal hobbies.

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:32.460
Respecting Uramichi Oniisan's taste,

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:36.630
I'll try submitting a proposal for
an Uramichi Oniisan dress-up figure.

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:39.180
In what demographic would
there even be a demand for that?

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:41.760
There's probably no demand
from any demographic.

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:45.180
At any rate... I'm a little shocked.

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:48.310
I never imagined that
Uramichi-<i>san </i>had such a hobby...

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:51.900
I guess there are things I don't notice
even though we hang out so often.

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:55.280
I don't know how I should act
when I see him tomorrow.

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.740
Yeah... I guess...

00:20:57.740 --> 00:20:59.700
I kind of sobered up.

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.280
Nekota. Give me some sweet booze.

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:03.530
Sure.

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:11.790
Hello, kids! How are you?!

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:13.250
Hey, Oniisan!

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:15.670
Hm? What's up?

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:20.220
My grandma bought this dress
for me. What do you think?

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:25.810
Yeah, it's very cute with lots of frills!

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:31.850
Usao-<i>kun</i>. You've been acting
weird today. What's the matter?

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:35.610
Ack! N-N-Nothing, hop!

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:42.490
Okay! We're done for the day!
Thank you for your hard work!

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:47.580
Hello.

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:49.040
Hey.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:53.540
Did your niece like your
birthday present for her?

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:57.550
Oh, yeah. Thanks for
checking things for me.

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:00.260
I didn't quite get it,
but she really liked it.

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:08.100
By the way, Usahara has been cold
and distant all day today.

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:08.100
Hm?

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:09.390
Do you know what it's about?

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:11.520
Seriously, Uramichi-<i>san</i>?

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:12.310
Seriously...?

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:13.140
Not really.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:16.190
Seriously, for real? Ahh...
